Apache Grails IntelliJ Plugin Installation Guide
================================================

This document covers building the Apache Grails IntelliJ Plugin from a source
distribution. Most users should instead install the plugin from the JetBrains
Marketplace inside IntelliJ IDEA.

Requirements
------------
To run the plugin:

        IntelliJ IDEA Ultimate 2026.2 or newer. The plugin depends on
        Ultimate-only functionality and will not load in IDEA Community.

To build the plugin:

        JDK      Liberica 25 (25.0.3-librca), pinned in `.sdkmanrc`
        Gradle   9.3.1, pinned in `.sdkmanrc`
        Disk     ~10 GB free for the IntelliJ Platform and Gradle caches
        Memory   8 GB or more; the build runs a 4 GB Gradle daemon
        Network  access to Maven Central and the JetBrains repositories

Building with a JDK other than the pinned one will usually work, but the
resulting artifacts will not be bit-for-bit reproducible against the official
release. See RELEASE.md for details.

1. Install the JDK and Gradle
-----------------------------
The required versions are pinned in `.sdkmanrc`, a configuration file for
SDKMAN! (https://sdkman.io). If SDKMAN! is installed, install and select both
tools by running the following at the source root:

        sdk env install

Otherwise install the same versions by any means you prefer -- for example
Liberica JDK 25 (https://bell-sw.com/pages/downloads/) and Gradle 9.3.1
(https://gradle.org/releases/) -- and put them on PATH:

        export JAVA_HOME=/path/to/liberica-jdk-25
        export PATH="$JAVA_HOME/bin:/path/to/gradle-9.3.1/bin:$PATH"

Confirm the versions before continuing:

        java -version
        gradle --version

2. Bootstrap the Gradle Wrapper
-------------------------------
Per ASF source-release policy this distribution ships without the Gradle
Wrapper jar, so it must be regenerated once using the Gradle installed above:

        cd gradle-bootstrap
        gradle bootstrap
        cd ..

`gradle-bootstrap` is a small standalone Gradle build whose only job is to run
the `wrapper` task at the version named in `.sdkmanrc` and copy the results
(`gradlew`, `gradlew.bat`, and `gradle/wrapper/`) to the source root.

Use `./gradlew` for every command below. It is preferred over invoking `gradle`
directly because it guarantees the pinned Gradle version is used.

3. Build
--------
Build the plugin distribution ZIP:

        ./gradlew buildPlugin

The ZIP is written to `build/distributions/` and can be installed in IntelliJ
IDEA via Settings > Plugins > Install Plugin from Disk.

Note: the first build downloads the IntelliJ Platform (IntelliJ IDEA Ultimate)
and the bundled plugins it compiles against, which takes time and several
gigabytes of disk space. Later builds reuse that cache.

To build a specific version instead of the default in `gradle.properties`:

        ./gradlew buildPlugin -Pversion=262.0.1

4. Verify (optional)
--------------------
Other useful tasks:

        ./gradlew check          # compile and run tests
        ./gradlew verifyPlugin   # IntelliJ Plugin Verifier
        ./gradlew rat            # Apache RAT license audit
        ./gradlew runIde         # launch a sandbox IDE with the plugin

To verify a staged or released distribution -- checksums, GPG signatures, and a
reproducible rebuild -- see RELEASE.md and the scripts under `etc/bin`.
